From eef7b093d796a50b1e6e658f0016d3addff27ee5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yasuhiro Niji <ysniji@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 07:56:14 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] [de] Set ideographs and kana upright in East Asian vertical
 text

A table cell with w:textDirection="tbRl" and a shape with vert="eaVert"
laid their text out as horizontal lines and drew them turned by 90
degrees, so every Japanese character lay on its side. Word writes tbRl
for Japanese vertical writing (MS-OE376 notes that it uses tbRlV for
another purpose) and sets ideographs, kana and full-width forms upright
in it; Latin letters, brackets, dashes and the prolonged sound mark
stay turned with the line, which gives them their vertical shape.

The container that turns its text now tells the text drawing the
transform it draws with. A character that stands upright in vertical
text (UAX #50 Vertical_Orientation U and Tu) is drawn turned back about
the centre of its em square; the comma and the full stop also move to
the top right of their square, where they sit in vertical text. Layout,
selection and the cursor are unchanged.

Vertical writing for a whole section is not part of this change: the
section's text direction does not reach sdkjs.

Assisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5
